.TH "MrmOpenHierarchyFromBufferWithSize" "library call"
.SH "NAME"
\fBMrmOpenHierarchyFromBufferWithSize\fP \(em Allocates a hierarchy ID and opens a buffer of known size containing a memory image of a UID file
.iX "MrmOpenHierarchyFrom\\%BufferWithSize"
.iX "uil functions" "MrmOpenHierarchyFrom\\%BufferWithSize"
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
.nf
#include <Mrm/MrmPublic\&.h>
.sp \n(PDu
\fBCardinal \fBMrmOpenHierarchyFromBufferWithSize\fP\fR(
\fBunsigned char *\fBuid_buffer\fR\fR,
\fBsize_t \fBuid_buffer_size\fR\fR,
\fBMrmHierarchy *\fBhierarchy_id\fR\fR);
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBMrmOpenHierarchyFromBufferWithSize\fP allows you to specify a buffer
containing the memory image of a UID file that MRM searches in
subsequent fetch operations\&.
This function also allocates a hierarchy ID and initializes the
optimized search lists in the hierarchy\&.
.PP
It is identical to \fBMrmOpenHierarchyFromBuffer\fP, except that it
is also given the size of the buffer\&.
MRM never reads outside the buffer, and the fetch functions report
an error rather than read past its end when the UID image is
truncated or its internal record pointers are corrupt\&.
Use this function instead of \fBMrmOpenHierarchyFromBuffer\fP whenever
the buffer comes from a source that is not fully trusted, such as a
file read into memory or data received from another program\&.
.PP
MRM does not copy the buffer\&.
It must remain valid and unchanged until the hierarchy is closed with
\fBMrmCloseHierarchy\fP\&.
.IP "\fIuid_buffer\fP" 10
Specifies a stream of bytes containing the memory image of a UID
file, as written by the UIL compiler\&.
.IP "\fIuid_buffer_size\fP" 10
Specifies the number of bytes in \fIuid_buffer\fP\&.
.IP "\fIhierarchy_id\fP" 10
Returns the search hierarchy ID\&.
The search hierarchy ID identifies the buffer that MRM searches when
performing subsequent fetch calls\&.
.SH "RETURN"
.PP
This function returns one of the following status return constants:
.iX "MrmSUCCESS"
.iX "MrmNOT_VALID"
.iX "MrmFAILURE"
.IP "\fBMrmSUCCESS\fP" 10
The function executed successfully\&.
.IP "\fBMrmNOT_VALID\fP" 10
The buffer is empty or too small to hold a UID file, or its header
is not valid\&.
.IP "\fBMrmFAILURE\fP" 10
The function failed\&.
.PP
Other failure status codes, such as \fBMrmBAD_RECORD\fP, are returned
when the buffer does not hold a valid UID file\&.
.SH "RELATED"
.PP
\fBMrmCloseHierarchy\fP(3),
\fBMrmOpenHierarchyFromBuffer\fP(3) and
\fBMrmOpenHierarchyPerDisplay\fP(3)\&.
